## Purpose

Stacked enforcement slice on top of PR #166.

PR #166 introduced the pure provider-neutral authorization contract:

`CapabilityGrant -> AuthorizationRequest -> AuthorizationDecision`.

This PR binds that contract into the existing mutation guard without changing all legacy consumers at once.

## Implementation

- `LoopGuard.evaluate_authorized(...)`
  - runs the existing structural/revision/budget/reconciliation/loop checks first;
  - requires one concrete active `CapabilityGrant`;
  - requires the grant ref to be active in current `WorkingState.capability_grant_refs`;
  - binds request task/principal/capability/delegation/environment/evidence scope to the current state and exact `AttemptIntent`;
  - requires `AuthorizationRequest.attempt_authorization_fingerprint == AttemptIntent.authorization_fingerprint`;
  - invokes Core `authorize_request()` for exact grant/action/resource matching;
  - returns only normal `GuardDecision ALLOW/BLOCK`.

- `WorkingState.record_authorized_attempt(...)`
  - records history/budgets only after `evaluate_authorized()` succeeds;
  - blocked authorization leaves WorkingState unchanged.

- Existing `record_attempt()` is intentionally retained as a legacy structural-guard path for staged consumer migration. Core v1 is not frozen until consequence-bearing consumers have moved off that path or are explicitly proven non-consequence-bearing.

## Security shape

The final authorization decision remains in project Core. Provider availability, backend success, MCP credentials, environmental content and constructed tool output cannot activate a grant.

Capability-specific adapters still own the mapping from native operations to bounded semantic `action_ref` / `resource_scope_ref`; Core does not learn selectors, HWNDs, file globs, URLs or provider tool names.

## Tests

Adversarial tests cover:

- missing grant;
- inactive grant ref;
- stale/mismatched exact AttemptIntent fingerprint;
- task/principal/capability/delegation/environment/evidence mismatches;
- action not granted;
- resource mismatch;
- blocked attempt causing zero WorkingState history mutation;
- structural LoopGuard failure taking precedence over authorization.

## Sequencing

This PR is stacked:

`#159 -> #165 -> #166 -> this PR`

It remains Draft and must not merge ahead of the prerequisite chain. The next slice after this one is migration of the first real mutating consumer onto `record_authorized_attempt()`, with capability-specific behavioral/physical acceptance where required.
